  "worked_solution": "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}\u2061x+{cos}^{2}\u2061x=1\\,\u27f9\\,{cos}^{2}\u2061x=1-{sin}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{{cos}^{2}\u2061x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({sec}^{2}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x+\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
